\title{Reciprocal-Zero Harmonics:\\Finite Spectral Bounds, Conjugation Symmetry, and Quadratic Rationality}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 21, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a finite reciprocal-spectral statistic motivated by attempts to interpret number-theoretic spectra as musical harmonies. For a finite set $Z$ of complex spectral parameters, define $H(Z)=\sum_{z\in Z}z^{-1}$. We prove that separation $|z|\geq\delta>0$ implies $|H(Z)|\leq |Z|/\delta$, so every point-count estimate transfers directly to the harmonic. Conjugation closure forces $H(Z)$ to be real. For distinct roots $\alpha,\beta$ satisfying $\alpha+\beta=\ell$ and $\alpha\beta=q\neq0$, the harmonic equals $\ell/q$; rational quadratic coefficients therefore produce a rational value. We also diagnose proposed small-cutoff assignments: an empty window has harmonic zero, so empty windows at cutoffs $2$ and $3$ coincide; the first cannot have value $1$, and the second cannot be transcendental. These results separate universal finite algebra from analytic hypotheses and provide a foundation for exact algorithms, graph-zeta examples, and renormalized extensions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
The Riemann zeta function connects prime numbers with complex analysis through
\[
\zeta(s)=\prod_p(1-p^{-s})^{-1},
\]
initially for $\operatorname{Re}(s)>1$ and then by meromorphic continuation. Its nontrivial zeros form a complex spectrum. This encourages a musical analogy: regard each zero as a partial and a finite spectral window as a chord. If a cutoff $T$ selects zeros $\rho$ satisfying $|\operatorname{Im}\rho|\leq T$, the proposed statistic is
\[
H(T)=\sum_{|\operatorname{Im}\rho|\leq T}\frac1\rho.
\]

Before assigning musical intervals to such values, one must ask whether the window contains any points, whether the sum is real, and what controls its magnitude. We answer these questions for arbitrary finite spectra. The framework yields four conclusions: separation and cardinality control magnitude; conjugation symmetry forces reality; empty windows invalidate proposed values at cutoffs $2$ and $3$; and quadratic factors give exact rational harmonics through Vieta's formulas.

The logarithmic-over-logarithmic estimate discussed below is a transfer principle under a stated counting hypothesis. It is not an unconditional count for ordinary zeta zeros, whose classical counting function has scale $T\log T$. Likewise, approximate zero tables motivate empty small windows but do not replace a certified zero-exclusion theorem. Our methodology is therefore support-first: certify which points occur, verify nonvanishing and symmetry, apply counting estimates, and only then classify the value arithmetically.

\section{Finite spectral windows}

\begin{definition}[Reciprocal harmonic]
Let $Z\subset\C$ be finite and contain no zero. Its reciprocal harmonic is
\[
\Harm(Z)=\sum_{z\in Z}\frac1z.
\]
A window family is a sequence $(Z_n)_{n\geq0}$ of such finite sets.
\end{definition}

The set convention counts repeated values once. When spectral multiplicity matters, one should use a multiset; the magnitude and conjugation arguments remain unchanged when occurrences are counted separately.

\begin{definition}[Separation from zero]
A finite window $Z$ is $\delta$-separated from the origin if $\delta>0$ and $|z|\geq\delta$ for every $z\in Z$.
\end{definition}

This is radial separation, not pairwise separation. It is precisely the condition needed to bound reciprocal magnitudes.

\begin{definition}[Conjugation closure]
A finite window $Z$ is conjugation-closed if
\[
z\in Z\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \overline z\in Z
\]
for every $z\in\C$.
\end{definition}

A complex number $w$ is algebraic over $\Q$ if a nonzero polynomial in $\Q[X]$ vanishes at $w$; otherwise it is transcendental. In particular, zero is algebraic because it is a root of $X$.

\section{Magnitude bounds from counting}

\begin{theorem}[Separated-Window Bound]
Let $Z\subset\C\setminus\{0\}$ be finite. If $|z|\geq\delta$ for every $z\in Z$, where $\delta>0$, then
\[
|\Harm(Z)|\leq\frac{\card Z}{\delta}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For every $z\in Z$, $|1/z|=1/|z|\leq1/\delta$. Hence the triangle inequality gives
\[
|\Harm(Z)|\leq\sum_{z\in Z}\frac1{|z|}
\leq\sum_{z\in Z}\frac1\delta
=\frac{\card Z}{\delta}.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Counting-to-Harmonic Transfer]
Let $(Z_n)_{n\geq0}$ be finite complex windows. Suppose a fixed $\delta>0$ satisfies $|z|\geq\delta$ for every $n$ and every $z\in Z_n$. If $B:\N\to\mathbb R$ satisfies $\card{Z_n}\leq B(n)$, then
\[
|\Harm(Z_n)|\leq\frac{B(n)}\delta
\]
for every $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply the Separated-Window Bound to $Z_n$ and insert $\card{Z_n}\leq B(n)$. Multiplication by the positive number $1/\delta$ preserves the inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Conditional logarithmic-over-logarithmic bound]
Under uniform separation, suppose that for some constant $C$ and every index in the intended range,
\[
\card{Z_n}\leq C\frac{\log n}{\log\log n}.
\]
Then
\[
|\Harm(Z_n)|\leq\frac C\delta\frac{\log n}{\log\log n}.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Use the preceding theorem with $B(n)=C\log n/\log\log n$.
\end{proof}

The range qualification avoids the singularity where $\log\log n=0$ and the sign change at small $n$. In asymptotic applications one works with sufficiently large $n$.

This corollary transfers, rather than proves, the counting hypothesis. For the standard zeta-zero count, the Riemann--von Mangoldt leading scale is
\[
N(T)\sim\frac{T}{2\pi}\log\frac{T}{2\pi}.
\]
Thus a smaller $\log T/\log\log T$ count must not be silently assumed for the full height window. It may instead describe a sparse subwindow or a differently indexed spectrum. Refined estimates for the full spectrum should exploit cancellation.

\section{Reality from conjugation symmetry}

\begin{theorem}[Conjugation-Symmetry Theorem]
If $Z\subset\C\setminus\{0\}$ is finite and conjugation-closed, then $\Harm(Z)$ is real; equivalently,
\[
\operatorname{Im}\Harm(Z)=0.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Conjugation commutes with inversion away from zero, so
\[
\overline{\Harm(Z)}
=\sum_{z\in Z}\frac1{\overline z}.
\]
Conjugation permutes $Z$, and reindexing gives $\overline{\Harm(Z)}=\Harm(Z)$. A complex number equal to its conjugate is real. Equivalently, every nonreal pair contributes
\[
\frac1z+\frac1{\overline z}=2\operatorname{Re}\left(\frac1z\right),
\]
while real points contribute real reciprocals.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
For $Z=\{1+2i,1-2i,3\}$,
\[
\Harm(Z)=\frac1{1+2i}+\frac1{1-2i}+\frac13
=\frac25+\frac13=\frac{11}{15}.
\]
Conjugation symmetry guarantees reality; the rational coordinates further yield rationality.
\end{example}

The theorem concerns finite sums. For an expanding infinite spectrum, convergence, ordering, and renormalization remain additional analytic problems.

\section{Empty-window diagnosis}

\begin{proposition}[Empty-Window Harmonic]
If $Z=\varnothing$, then $\Harm(Z)=0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The sum indexed by the empty set is the additive identity.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Failure of the unit assignment]
If the spectral window at cutoff $2$ is empty, its harmonic is $0$ and therefore is not $1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Coincidence of empty small windows]
If the windows at cutoffs $2$ and $3$ are empty, then
\[
H(2)=H(3)=0.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Failure of the transcendence assignment]
If the spectral window at cutoff $3$ is empty, its harmonic is not transcendental over $\Q$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The value is zero, and the nonzero rational polynomial $X$ vanishes at zero. Thus zero is algebraic over $\Q$.
\end{proof}

Numerical tables place the first nontrivial zeta zeros at imaginary parts approximately $\pm14.1347$, indicating that ordinary symmetric height windows at $2$ and $3$ are empty. A numerical table, however, is not a self-contained zero-free proof. The exact conclusions above are conditional on emptiness; a complete zeta specialization needs certified inequalities excluding nontrivial zeros in the strip. Once emptiness is known, both proposed musical assignments fail before any subtle arithmetic classification begins.

\section{Quadratic spectra and rational harmonics}

\begin{theorem}[Quadratic Reciprocal-Harmonic Identity]
Let $\alpha,\beta,\ell,q\in\C$ satisfy
\[
\alpha+\beta=\ell,\qquad \alpha\beta=q,\qquad q\neq0,
\]
and suppose $\alpha\neq\beta$ so that the set $\{\alpha,\beta\}$ contains both roots. Then
\[
\Harm(\{\alpha,\beta\})=\frac\ell q.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $\alpha\beta=q\neq0$, both roots are nonzero. Therefore
\[
\Harm(\{\alpha,\beta\})
=\frac1\alpha+\frac1\beta
=\frac{\alpha+\beta}{\alpha\beta}
=\frac\ell q.
\]
\end{proof}

The distinctness condition is needed only because a set counts a repeated root once. With multiset semantics, the identity remains true with multiplicity for a repeated root.

\begin{corollary}[Rational quadratic harmonics]
Under the preceding hypotheses, if $\ell,q\in\Q$, then the harmonic is rational and equals $\ell/q$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Factorization-and-Harmonic Principle]
Under the same hypotheses, for every $u\in\C$,
\[
1-\ell u+qu^2=(1-\alpha u)(1-\beta u),
\]
and the two-root reciprocal harmonic is $\ell/q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Expansion gives
\[
(1-\alpha u)(1-\beta u)
=1-(\alpha+\beta)u+\alpha\beta u^2
=1-\ell u+qu^2.
\]
The harmonic statement is the Quadratic Reciprocal-Harmonic Identity.
\end{proof}

Quadratic factors of this form occur in finite graph-zeta constructions. The result converts a spectral chord into a coefficient invariant and avoids explicit root extraction.

\begin{example}
For $\alpha=2$ and $\beta=3$, one has $\ell=5$ and $q=6$. Thus
\[
(1-2u)(1-3u)=1-5u+6u^2,
\qquad
\Harm(\{2,3\})=\frac12+\frac13=\frac56.
\]
\end{example}

\begin{example}
For $\alpha=1+2i$ and $\beta=1-2i$, one has $\ell=2$ and $q=5$, so
\[
\Harm(\{1+2i,1-2i\})=\frac25.
\]
Conjugation symmetry explains reality, while Vieta's identity explains rationality.
\end{example}

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Direct evaluation}
Given nonzero complex numbers $z_1,\ldots,z_m$, initialize $S=0$ and update $S\leftarrow S+1/z_j$ for each point. This takes $O(m)$ arithmetic operations and $O(1)$ auxiliary space when streamed. The input must be rejected if any point is zero. Compensated summation can reduce floating-point error; exact arithmetic is preferable for rational data.

\subsection{Cutoff filtering}
Given candidate spectral points and $T\geq0$, retain points satisfying $|\operatorname{Im}z|\leq T$ and evaluate their harmonic. A single query takes $O(m)$ time. For many cutoffs, sort once by $|\operatorname{Im}z|$ in $O(m\log m)$ time and construct prefix sums. This evaluates the supplied data but does not certify that the list is complete.

\subsection{Quadratic coefficient evaluation}
For $1-\ell u+qu^2$ with $q\neq0$, return $\ell/q$. This requires constant arithmetic time and avoids roots, square roots, and cancellation errors. Rational coefficients can be handled by exact fractions.

A responsible computational report should include the selected cardinality, minimum modulus, the bound $|Z|/\delta$, a conjugation diagnostic, and the real and imaginary parts of the sum. These quantities distinguish structural guarantees from floating-point coincidence.

\section{Applications and discussion}

The counting-transfer theorem applies to sparse eigenvalue bands, polynomial roots, and filtered graph spectra whenever an exclusion radius is known. Graph-zeta factors are especially suitable because their coefficients can often be computed exactly. Covers, products, and subdivisions may then be studied through changes in coefficient ratios.

The quadratic result is a special case of a wider principle. If nonzero roots $r_1,\ldots,r_d$ are counted with multiplicity, then $\sum_j1/r_j$ is a ratio of elementary symmetric functions and hence a coefficient ratio, up to the polynomial's sign convention. Rationality is therefore often controlled by the coefficient field and Galois invariance rather than by mysterious properties of individual roots. Selected subsets of roots are subtler because they need not be Galois invariant.

The universal cardinality bound can be crude. A conjugate pair $\rho=\sigma+it$ and $\overline\rho=\sigma-it$ contributes
\[
\frac1\rho+\frac1{\overline\rho}
=\frac{2\sigma}{\sigma^2+t^2},
\]
which decays quadratically in $|t|$ when $\sigma$ is bounded. By contrast, summing reciprocal moduli sees only linear decay. Any refined zeta analysis should exploit this pairing.

An auditory display may map a real statistic $h$ to a frequency such as $f_0 2^h$ or to a pitch class modulo $1$. Such sonification is an external design choice, not a theorem about consonance. The present results calibrate its inputs: symmetry supplies real values, coefficient identities supply exact rationals, and counting supplies magnitude controls.

\section{Future work}

\paragraph{Renormalized convergence.}
Define a multiplicity-sensitive, conjugate-symmetric sum over zeta zeros and seek an explicit renormalization under which it converges. Quantitative errors should be derived from the Riemann--von Mangoldt formula and tested against certified zero tables.

\paragraph{Arithmetic classification.}
Classify algebraic root multisets whose reciprocal sums are rational, algebraic irrational, or transcendental. Higher-degree Euler factors and prescribed Galois groups offer natural test cases; one may ask whether rationality is equivalent to a Galois-invariant coefficient ratio under nonvanishing hypotheses.

\paragraph{Graph operations.}
Determine how products, covers, and edge subdivisions transform graph-zeta chord spectra. A concrete direction is to test whether rational chord spectra persist under finite regular covers precisely when traces and determinants of new factors remain rational.

\paragraph{Certified exclusion windows.}
Prove from explicit analytic inequalities that no nontrivial zeta zero satisfies $|\operatorname{Im}\rho|\leq14$. Combined with the Empty-Window Harmonic, this would settle every cutoff in that interval at once.

\paragraph{Prime-indexed bands.}
Replace a direct prime cutoff by a band whose endpoints depend on $\log p$, then test whether multiplication of primes corresponds to addition, convolution, or another operation on harmonic statistics. Any proposal should specify completeness, multiplicity, normalization, and a falsifiable composition law.

\section{Conclusion}
Finite reciprocal-zero harmonics obey clean structural laws. Separation transfers point counts to magnitude bounds. Conjugation closure makes the sum real. Empty windows have value zero, invalidating proposed unit and transcendental assignments at empty cutoffs $2$ and $3$. Quadratic factors instead yield exact values $\ell/q$, with rational coefficients producing rational harmonics.

The framework does not identify primes with musical intervals by decree. It supplies the calibration required to test such ideas: certify support, respect symmetry, separate hypotheses from conclusions, and compute symmetric invariants exactly. In that disciplined setting, number-theoretic and graph-theoretic spectra form a genuine laboratory for arithmetic harmony.

\end{document}

